<section aria-labelledby="three-labels"><h2 id="three-labels">Separate three kinds of product labels</h2>
<p>A label such as “¾-inch gravel,” a grading such as #57, and a regional name such as crusher run answer different questions. The first suggests a size, the second points to a grading system, and the third may describe a locally sold blend. None alone establishes the material’s mineral type, washing, bulk weight or correct use on your site.</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table><caption>Questions to ask about the words on an aggregate quote</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Label type</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Information still needed</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Nominal product size</th><td>¾-inch stone</td><td>Actual grading, oversize and smaller fractions, fines and wash status</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Numbered size designation</th><td>#57 or #67</td><td>Governing specification, sieve results, source and intended layer</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Minus product</th><td>¾-inch minus</td><td>The complete particle-size blend and whether its fines meet the project requirements</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Regional or supplier name</th><td>QP, DGA, 21-A or CA-6</td><td>Exact product designation; names from different agencies are not automatically interchangeable</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div></section>
<section aria-labelledby="sieves"><h2 id="sieves">ASTM D448 / AASHTO M43 coarse aggregate sieves</h2>
<p>Sieve analysis separates a sample through a series of square openings and reports the percentage passing each sieve. A larger opening passes more of the sample, while numbered sieves (such as No. 4, No. 8, or No. 200) use designated wire-cloth openings rather than inch fractions.</p>
<div class="table-responsive"><table><caption>Reference coarse aggregate size designations per ASTM D448 / AASHTO M43</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Size #</th><th scope="col">Nominal Size</th><th scope="col">Key Sieve Limits (% Passing)</th><th scope="col">Typical Project Uses</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">#1</th><td>3½ to 1½ in</td><td>90–100% passing 3½ in; 0–15% passing 1½ in</td><td>Heavy subbase stabilization, gabions, construction entrances</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#4</th><td>1½ to ¾ in</td><td>90–100% passing 1½ in; 0–15% passing ¾ in</td><td>Structural drainage courses, heavy backfill, primary reservoir layers</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#57</th><td>1 in to No. 4</td><td>95–100% passing 1 in; 25–60% passing ½ in; 0–10% passing No. 4</td><td>French drains, pipe bedding, concrete aggregate, clean driveways</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#67</th><td>¾ in to No. 4</td><td>90–100% passing ¾ in; 20–55% passing ⅜ in; 0–10% passing No. 4</td><td>Concrete mixes, tight utility trench bedding, clean architectural paths</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#78</th><td>½ in to No. 8</td><td>90–100% passing ½ in; 40–75% passing ⅜ in; 0–10% passing No. 8</td><td>Surface chip seals, thin top-dressing, fine drainage overlays</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#8</th><td>⅜ in to No. 8</td><td>85–100% passing ⅜ in; 10–30% passing No. 4; 0–10% passing No. 8</td><td>Pea gravel / fine walkways, chip seal, tightly spaced paver joints</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">#89</th><td>⅜ in to No. 16</td><td>90–100% passing ⅜ in; 20–55% passing No. 4; 5–30% passing No. 8</td><td>Masonry fills, thin ornamental beds, small aggregate concrete mixes</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Ask for the current grading sheet and the specification used to evaluate it. Compare the complete required sieve series, not only the largest stone visible on the pile. <a href="https://www.pa.gov/agencies/penndot/programs-and-doing-business/construction/aggregate-plant-book">PennDOT’s producer records</a> list separate grading worksheets for #57, #67, other coarse aggregates and base products. Its <a href="https://www.ecamms.penndot.pa.gov/Public/Pages/Bulletins/BulletinSearch.aspx?BulletinTypeKey=1">Bulletin 14 search</a> is a source for Pennsylvania aggregate approvals.</p></section>
<section id="57-stone" aria-labelledby="stone57-heading"><h2 id="stone57-heading">Understanding #57 stone before ordering</h2>
<p><strong>#57</strong> identifies a coarse aggregate grading in <a href="https://www.pa.gov/content/dam/copapwp-pagov/en/penndot/documents/public/pubsforms/publications/pub_408/408_2026/408_2026_ie.pdf">state highway</a> and ASTM D448 specifications. It is not a 57-millimeter particle diameter, a single mineral type, or a certification that every quarry load has been washed free of dust. One quarry sells crushed #57 limestone or granite; another sells rounded river stone meeting #57 sieve limits.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Source and shape:</strong> confirm mineral type (limestone, granite, trap rock, or rounded river stone).</li><li><strong>Fines and washing:</strong> request the No. 200 wash result when ordering for French drains or permeable bedding.</li><li><strong>Loose bulk weight:</strong> ask the supplier for pounds or short tons per cubic yard (many #57 clean stone quotes fall near 1.30–1.45 short tons/yd³).</li></ul>
<p>A 100 sq ft layer of #57 stone at 3 inches deep occupies 0.93 yd³ before extra; with a 10% allowance, order 1.02 yd³ (about 1.38 short tons at 1.35 tons/yd³). Estimate trench and pipe deductions on the <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain rock calculator</a> or calculate bulk tons on the <a href="/crushed-stone-calculator">crushed stone calculator</a>.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="fines"><h2 id="fines">Clean grading and dense grading serve different layers</h2>
<p>An open-graded product has relatively few smaller particles between the larger pieces. A dense-graded product includes a broader blend and fines that can help particles pack together. The actual grading and fines content affect performance along with shape, moisture, confinement and the ground underneath.</p>
<p>Confirm washing and measured fines separately. A size number does not guarantee that stone is washed or establish its drainage rate. For a drain, follow the pipe and filter detail; for a base, follow the required product, thickness and placement procedure. The <a href="/angular-vs-rounded-rock">particle-shape comparison</a> explains another part of that choice.</p></section>
<section aria-labelledby="order"><h2 id="order">Turn the product choice into a quantity</h2>
<ol><li>Identify the actual product and layer specification.</li><li>Measure the finished footprint and selected layer thickness.</li><li>Calculate yards from geometry, then add a clearly defined loose-order allowance if needed.</li><li>Convert yards to short tons with that supplier’s loose bulk weight, or use the bag’s stated cubic-foot yield.</li></ol>
<p>A 3-inch layer over 200 sq ft needs 1.85 yd³ before extra, whether the selected product is rounded or angular. Its estimated tons depend on loose bulk density. Particle size can change the thickness you need to select; it does not change the geometric conversion for a specified area and depth.</p>
<p>Use the <a href="/gravel-calculator">gravel calculator</a> for a loose layer, the <a href="/crusher-run-calculator">crusher run calculator</a> for a specified base, or check regional quarry codes in our <a href="/gravel-trade-names-by-state">aggregate trade names guide</a>.</p></section>

